bundle-plugin(1) -- Manage Bundler plugins
==========================================

## SYNOPSIS

`bundle plugin` install PLUGINS [--source=SOURCE] [--version=VERSION]
                              [--git=GIT] [--branch=BRANCH|--ref=REF]
                              [--path=PATH]<br>
`bundle plugin` uninstall PLUGINS [--all]<br>
`bundle plugin` list<br>
`bundle plugin` help [COMMAND]

## DESCRIPTION

You can install, uninstall, and list plugin(s) with this command to extend functionalities of Bundler.

## SUB-COMMANDS

### install

Install the given plugin(s).

For example, `bundle plugin install bundler-graph` will install bundler-graph
gem from globally configured sources (defaults to RubyGems.org). Note that the
global source specified in Gemfile is ignored.

**OPTIONS**

* `--source=SOURCE`:
  Install the plugin gem from a specific source, rather than from globally configured sources.

  Example: `bundle plugin install bundler-graph --source https://example.com`

* `--version=VERSION`:
  Specify a version of the plugin gem to install via `--version`.

  Example: `bundle plugin install bundler-graph --version 0.2.1`

* `--git=GIT`:
  Install the plugin gem from a Git repository. You can use standard Git URLs like:

  `ssh://[user@]host.xz[:port]/path/to/repo.git`<br>
  `http[s]://host.xz[:port]/path/to/repo.git`<br>
  `/path/to/repo`<br>
  `file:///path/to/repo`

  Example: `bundle plugin install bundler-graph --git https://github.com/rubygems/bundler-graph`

* `--branch=BRANCH`:
  When you specify `--git`, you can use `--branch`  to use.

* `--ref=REF`:
  When you specify `--git`, you can use `--ref` to specify any tag, or commit
  hash (revision) to use.

* `--path=PATH`:
  Install the plugin gem from a local path.

  Example: `bundle plugin install bundler-graph --path ../bundler-graph`

### uninstall

Uninstall the plugin(s) specified in PLUGINS.

**OPTIONS**

* `--all`:
  Uninstall all the installed plugins. If no plugin is installed, then it does nothing.

### list

List the installed plugins and available commands.

No options.

### help

Describe subcommands or one specific subcommand.

No options.

## HOOKS

A plugin can run code at defined points of Bundler's work by registering a
hook. In the plugin's `plugins.rb`, pass one of the event constants of
`Bundler::Plugin::Events` and a block to `Bundler::Plugin::API.hook`. Bundler
loads a plugin that registers a hook for an event when that event fires, and
calls the block with the arguments listed for the event below.

    Bundler::Plugin::API.hook Bundler::Plugin::Events::GEM_BEFORE_INSTALL_ALL do |dependencies|
      puts "about to install #{dependencies.map(&:name).join(", ")}"
    end

The events fire in the order listed here.

**GEMFILE EVALUATION**

* `GEM_BEFORE_EVAL` (`before-eval`):
  Called before the Gemfile is evaluated, with the path to the Gemfile and
  the path to the lockfile (`nil` when there is no lockfile).

* `GEM_AFTER_EVAL` (`after-eval`):
  Called after the Gemfile is evaluated, with the resulting
  `Bundler::Definition`.

**INSTALLATION**

* `GEM_BEFORE_INSTALL_ALL` (`before-install-all`):
  Called before any gem is installed, with an Array of the
  `Bundler::Dependency` objects declared in the Gemfile.

* `GEM_BEFORE_FETCH` (`before-fetch`):
  Called before each individual gem is downloaded from a remote source, with
  a spec-like object responding to the `Gem::Specification` API (for example
  a `Bundler::EndpointSpecification` or `Bundler::RemoteSpecification`).
  Does not fire for a gem that is already present in the download cache.

* `GEM_AFTER_FETCH` (`after-fetch`):
  Called after each individual gem is downloaded from a remote source, with
  the same spec-like object as `GEM_BEFORE_FETCH`. Also fires when the
  download fails.

* `GIT_BEFORE_FETCH` (`before-git-fetch`):
  Called before a git source is fetched or checked out, with the
  `Bundler::Source::Git` being fetched.

* `GIT_AFTER_FETCH` (`after-git-fetch`):
  Called after a git source is fetched or checked out, with the same
  `Bundler::Source::Git`. Also fires when the fetch or checkout fails.

* `GEM_BEFORE_INSTALL` (`before-install`):
  Called before each individual gem is installed, with a
  `Bundler::ParallelInstaller::SpecInstallation`. Its `state`, `error` and
  `post_install_message` are not set yet, as nothing has been installed.

* `GEM_AFTER_INSTALL` (`after-install`):
  Called after each individual gem is installed, with the same
  `Bundler::ParallelInstaller::SpecInstallation`. If its `state` is
  `failed`, `error` holds the exception; if the `state` is `success`, a
  `post_install_message` may be present.

* `GEM_AFTER_INSTALL_ALL` (`after-install-all`):
  Called after all gems are installed, with an Array of the
  `Bundler::Dependency` objects declared in the Gemfile.

**REQUIRE**

These events fire from `Bundler.require`.

* `GEM_BEFORE_REQUIRE_ALL` (`before-require-all`):
  Called before any gem is required, with an Array of the
  `Bundler::Dependency` objects being required.

* `GEM_BEFORE_REQUIRE` (`before-require`):
  Called before each individual gem is required, with its
  `Bundler::Dependency`.

* `GEM_AFTER_REQUIRE` (`after-require`):
  Called after each individual gem is required, with its
  `Bundler::Dependency`.

* `GEM_AFTER_REQUIRE_ALL` (`after-require-all`):
  Called after all gems are required, with an Array of the
  `Bundler::Dependency` objects that were required.

